data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Natural origin and core mechanism:** CRISPR was first found in bacteria, where it defends against viruses [1]. A guide RNA locates target DNA, and Cas9 binds and cuts it, which can switch off the gene [3].\n\n- **Gene editing applications:** Scientists often knock out genes by breaking them so the cell stops making the protein [2]. Adding repair templates allows disease-causing mutations to be replaced with healthy sequences [2].\n\n- **Expanding research use:** Synthetic guide RNAs let Cas9 cut the genome at chosen locations [7]. CRISPR has also been used to study HIV in cell culture [6]."}

